# ─── Nox PiDog Network Config ───
# Zentrale Netzwerk-Konfiguration fuer alle PiDog-Services.
# EDIT THIS FILE BEFORE STARTING THE SERVICES — the values below are examples.
# Bei IP-Aenderung nur DIESE Datei anpassen, dann:
#   sudo systemctl daemon-reload && sudo systemctl restart nox-bridge nox-voice
#
# Brain = the machine running the brain (Pi 5, desktop, ...).
# Use its LAN or Tailscale IP; 127.0.0.1 only if brain and body
# run on the SAME machine (i.e. on the robot itself).
BRAIN_HOST=192.168.1.100
BRAIN_CALLBACK_PORT=8889
#
# Clawdbot Gateway (fuer Voice Loop v1 Fallback) — optional, same host as brain
CLAWDBOT_HOST=192.168.1.100
CLAWDBOT_PORT=18789
#
# ─── Autonomous behavior ───
# The bridge starts Nox's behavior engine at boot: the dog idles, patrols and
# plays on its own. Uncomment to disable it — the dog then only moves on your
# commands (it can still be started on demand: POST /behavior/start).
# The LEDs keep breathing either way: the daemon uses them as a status light.
# Apply with: sudo systemctl restart nox-bridge
#NOX_NO_AUTO=1
#
# ─── Audio output ───
# Unset (recommended): sound goes to the ALSA default, which SunFounder's own
# installer configures — on the PiDog that is the robot_hat speaker, which
# registers as a "Google voiceHAT" sound card.
# AUDIODEV=auto        → detect a speaker card, skipping HDMI outputs
# AUDIODEV=plughw:2,0  → pin one card; check the numbers with: aplay -l
# The daemon logs which device it uses and why, and refuses a card that does
# not exist instead of playing into the void.
#AUDIODEV=auto
#
# ─── Voice (Piper TTS for /speak) ───
# Unset (recommended): /speak uses any installed piper voice — de_DE-thorsten-high
# if present, otherwise the first one found in ~/.local/share/piper-voices or
# ~/.piper_models (where SunFounder's examples put theirs). Each voice is a pair:
# <name>.onnx + <name>.onnx.json, from https://huggingface.co/rhasspy/piper-voices
# Pin one (e.g. a French voice) with the full path to the .onnx file:
#PIPER_MODEL=/home/pi/.piper_models/fr_FR-siwis-medium.onnx
# The piper program itself is found on PATH or in ~/.local/bin; pin it only if not:
#PIPER_BIN=/usr/local/bin/piper
# Language of what the dog says on its own (battery warnings): de, en or fr.
# Unset: the language of the voice above (fr_FR-... → fr), otherwise German.
#NOX_LANG=fr
#
# ─── Voice input (speaking to the dog — Vosk STT, optional) ───
# The nox-voice service listens on a USB microphone for the wake word ("Nox")
# and sends what you say to the brain. It needs a Vosk model; without one the
# service exits cleanly and voice input stays off (everything else works).
# 1. Pick a model for YOUR language — it decides what the dog understands:
#    https://alphacephei.com/vosk/models   (e.g. vosk-model-small-fr-0.22,
#    vosk-model-small-en-us-0.15; default: vosk-model-small-de-0.15)
# 2. Download and unzip it:  mkdir -p ~/vosk-models && cd ~/vosk-models
#    wget https://alphacephei.com/vosk/models/vosk-model-small-fr-0.22.zip && unzip *.zip
# 3. Point this at the unzipped FOLDER, then: sudo systemctl restart nox-voice
#VOSK_MODEL_PATH=/home/pi/vosk-models/vosk-model-small-fr-0.22
#
# ─── Storage locations (optional) ───
# Where registered faces are kept (photos + embeddings):
#NOX_FACE_DB_DIR=/home/pi/nox_face_db
# Where the dog's long-term memory lives:
#PIDOG_MEMORY_DIR=/home/pi/memory
#
# ─── Access to the bridge (port 8888) ───
# The bridge moves the robot. Requests from the robot itself (localhost) are
# always allowed; requests from other machines — and anything arriving through
# a tunnel or proxy on the robot — need this token once it is set:
#   Authorization: Bearer <token>
# Set the same value as NOX_API_TOKEN on the brain (/etc/default/nox-brain).
# Generate one: python3 -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))"
#NOX_API_TOKEN=
# Requests per minute per remote address (default 600, 0 = no limit):
#NOX_RATE_LIMIT=600
